#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222406} :activity/id #uuid "92d0371b-d4af-52c0-a1d3-83d95f7cf2d7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048912 :topic/id #uuid "cbc6dbdd-cff6-5796-8970-556d22f811ac" :topic/math-academy-id 6397} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222406 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222483 :tutorial/content "For a first-order initial value problem, we have already seen ways to approximate the increment in $y$ over a step, including:\n\n- the modified Euler method, which predicts using Euler's method and then corrects that prediction, and\n\n- the RK4 method, which combines several slope evaluations within a single step.\n\nAnother group of methods, called the **Adams-Bashforth-Moulton methods**, takes a different approach. Rather than computing multiple slopes at new points, these methods reuse slopes from previous steps in a predictor-corrector framework, giving high accuracy with relatively little additional work.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll focus on one of the simplest: the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the **$2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method** (**ABM $2$-step**) with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the slope at the current point $(x,y),$ and\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ where $x_{-1} = x-\\Delta x.$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by $y_p = y + \\Delta y_p.$\n\nSince ABM $2$-step uses the slope from the previous step, it cannot be started from a single initial condition alone. \nInstead, we initialize the method by computing the first step with a one-step method, typically the RK4 method.\n\nOnce the prediction $y_p$ is computed, we apply a corrector equation to refine our approximation. We'll return to this step shortly.\nBefore that, let's see an example of how to apply the predictor equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "50a2f37b-38d8-5a6f-bc13-9086af674ed7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14412 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5af445aa-9ec9-5489-9176-c4332bff0a03" :step/math-academy-id 611738 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222407}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222510 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x - 2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nLet's begin the process of applying the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nWe know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1).$ Since the method takes $2$ steps (one to initialize, one to approximate), we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nWe will use a table to keep track of known values. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we initialize the ABM $2$-step method by making a preliminary step with a one-step method.\n\nApplying the RK4 method with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12,$ we obtain the slope $y'=-2,$ increment $\\Delta y = -\\dfrac14,$ and approximation\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx 1 + \\left(-\\dfrac14\\right) = \\dfrac34.\n$$\n\nWe add the slope to the table, and the approximation in a new row.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |  |  |\n\nNote that, since RK4 is not a predictor-corrector method, there is no increment $\\Delta y_p$ or predictor $y_p$ for this step. So, we leave these entries in the first row blank.\n\nNow, we continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |\n\nTherefore, our initial prediction for $y(1)$ is $y_p = \\dfrac{13}8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c403981-071d-5b27-8ff8-99c1ea98715b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14615 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "fb2bf195-d713-5c11-b38c-0b93be4ea2b8" :step/math-academy-id 611739 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222408}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221458 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221161 :question/id #uuid "c736760f-414e-5ab2-adfa-c09acce10d5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21539"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "874de02f-c5be-54ed-8d66-a3b1f1f23c9a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050891}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308373}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table Using the Predictor Equation of the ABM 2-Step Method"} :step/id #uuid "183435e5-3131-5485-8b09-dfbc5b6bc6b9" :step/math-academy-id 611740 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222409}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222409 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222479 :tutorial/content "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe **$2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton** (**ABM $2$-step**) **Method** with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y' = f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- $y'_{-1} = f(x_{-1}, y_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}),$ and\n\n- $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new}, y_p)$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nThe final approximation for the new step is $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y.$\n\nSo, the corrector of the ABM $2$-step combines the slope evaluated at the predicted point with the slope evaluated at the current point and the previous point. Let's demonstrate how to apply the corrector equation to our previous example." :tutorial/id #uuid "684ff8c6-ca91-5e58-82e6-41d645b1e6db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14372 :tutorial/title "The Two-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method"} :step/id #uuid "78553a6e-4fa7-55a6-9f7b-b53ad1a3a237" :step/math-academy-id 611743 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222410}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222410 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222480 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x-2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nWe will now complete our approximation of the value of $y(1)$ using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method.\n\nFirst, recall that we previously initialized with the RK4 method and completed the predictor step of the ABM $2$-step method, resulting in the following values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |  |  |\n\nNow, the slope at the point $(x_\\text{new},y_p) = \\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{13}8\\right) = \\left(1, \\dfrac{13}8\\right)$ estimated by the predictor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{13}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x, y) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac34\\right)$ is $y' = \\dfrac12.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{39}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n\nWe know the value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = \\dfrac34 + \\dfrac{13}{32} = \\dfrac{37}{32}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac{13}{32}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\dfrac{37}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx \\dfrac{37}{32}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "098a5bb7-0ece-59ec-8400-a8e3efe1b00a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14385 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "702af9a1-f869-585a-9d62-8b4e15161407" :step/math-academy-id 611744 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222411}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222411 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221459 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221164 :question/id #uuid "a185d6d7-e351-5f24-891a-aeb318cef584" :question/math-academy-id "e-21565"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ecbd28c-9d5a-5802-8792-6ba97651ecd2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374683}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Corrector Term"} :step/id #uuid "f8c031d4-df22-535d-926c-b13ae32837bd" :step/math-academy-id 611745 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222412}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222412 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221460 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221160 :question/id #uuid "14669099-db40-5dd2-b4d5-4db016374643" :question/math-academy-id "e-21538"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5aefa9-a83c-5203-8b73-c96b5f7df6bf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374711}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using the ABM 2-Step Method"} :step/id #uuid "a7c6fe9a-69d3-5418-8b88-993af0fa999e" :step/math-academy-id 611748 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222413}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221461 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221165 :question/id #uuid "ab9a026d-ddb2-52e8-9f52-4fbcf9b482a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21566"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5a37e3d-f1ce-57f2-baef-c464a4f89ad4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398774}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using The ABM 2-Step Method"} :step/id #uuid "40865e2e-2c40-5544-acb9-196c8c726a9f" :step/math-academy-id 611751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222414}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222414 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222485 :tutorial/content "Where do the predictor and corrector equations of the two-step Adams–Bashforth–Moulton method come from?\n\nThe method consists of two parts: the **Adams-Bashforth** (**AB2**) **predictor**, and **Adams-Moulton** (**AM2**) **corrector**.\nWe'll begin by deriving the AB2 predictor.\n\nConsider an initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a)=c\n$$\n\nWriting the differential equation in integral form, we have\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet $\\Delta x = x_\\text{new} - x$ be the step size, and define the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t - x}{\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThen, $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nNow, define a new function $g$ of $\\theta$ as the integrand:\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulas result from approximating $g(\\theta)$ by a simple function that agrees with its known values, and then integrating that approximation from $0$ to $1.$ We now determine some known values.\n\n- Evaluating $g$ at $\\theta = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =f(x+0\\cdot Δx,y(x+0\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x,y) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, evaluating $g$ at $\\theta=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =f(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx,y(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x-Δx,y(x-Δx)) \\\\  & =f({x}_{-1},y({x}_{-1})) \\\\  & ={y}_{-1}^{'},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_{-1}$ is the previous $x$-value and $y'_{-1}$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).$\n\nSince we have two known values, the simplest reasonable approximation to $g(\\theta)$ is a *straight line* passing through these two points: $(0,y')$ and $(-1,y'_{-1}).$\nThis straight line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\theta ) & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}}{0-(-1)}(\\theta -0) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by integrating this approximation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta )\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={({y}^{'}\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}){\\theta }^{2})|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0}^{\\theta \\,=\\,1} \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy={\\int }_{0}^{1}g(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx\\approx {\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we define as the AB2 predictor:\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulae result from approximating [math]g(\\theta)[/math] by a polynomial interpolant constructed from known function values and integrating that polynomial from [math]0[/math] to [math]1.[/math]\n\nNotice that evaluating [math]g[/math] at [math]\\theta = 0,[/math] we have\n\\begin{align*}\ng(0) &= f(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x, y) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSimilarly, evaluating [math]g[/math] at [math]\\theta=-1[/math], we get\n\\begin{align*}\ng(-1)\n&= f(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x - \\Delta x, y(x - \\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'_{-1},\n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere [math]y'_{-1}[/math] is the slope at the previous point [math](x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).[/math]\nSo, to derive the AB2 predictor, we can approximate [math]g(\\theta)[/math] by a *linear* interpolant using its values at [math]\\theta=0[/math] and [math]\\theta=-1.[/math]\n\nThe Lagrange basis polynomials are\n\\[\n\\ell_0(\\theta) = \\theta + 1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) = -\\theta,\n\\]\n\nso the interpolant is\n\\begin{align*}\np(\\theta) &= g(0)\\ell_0(\\theta) - g(-1)\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta.\n\\end{align*}\n\nFinally, by integrating the interpolant, we obtain\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\n&= \\int_0^1 \\big(y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta\\big) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\int_0^1 (\\theta + 1) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta - y'_{-1} \\int_0^1 \\theta \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2 + \\theta\\right]_0^1 - y'_{-1}\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2\\right]_0^1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left(\\dfrac12 + 1 - 0\\right) - y'_{-1}\\left(\\dfrac12 - 0\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the AB2 predictor is\n\\begin{align*}\n\\Delta y \\approx \\Delta y_p &= \\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\cdot \\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\left(\\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}\\right)\\cdot\\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "dee56e00-8f80-598d-a5ea-b99a6322bddd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14478 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the AB2 Predictor"} :step/id #uuid "d6baca6f-a078-54e6-b8c4-a3e52eb82476" :step/math-academy-id 611754 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222415}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222415 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222486 :tutorial/content "So far, we have derived the AB2 predictor. We are left to derive the AM2 corrector.\n\nRecall that we wrote the differential equation in integral form as\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_x^{x_\\text{new}} f(t,y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWith the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t-x}{\\Delta x},\n$$\n\nwe had $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nand we defined\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x+\\theta\\Delta x,y(x+\\theta\\Delta x)).\n$$\n\nFor the predictor, we considered two known values of $g(\\theta){:}$ \n\n- For $\\theta = 0,$ we have $g(0) = y'.$\n\n- For $\\theta = -1,$ we have $g(-1) = y'_{-1}.$\n\nThen, we approximated $g$ with a linear function passing through these two points.\n\nNow, for the corrector, we use *three* known values, and approximate $g(\\theta)$ by a *quadratic* passing through these three points.\n\nTo obtain a third value, we also want the slope at the *new* $x$-value $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$ But, the true value $y_\\text{new}$ is not yet known.\n\nInstead, we evaluate the slope at the new point estimated by the AB2 predictor! This corresponds to $\\theta = 1{:}$\n\n$$\ng(1) = f(x + \\Delta x, y(x + \\Delta x)) \\approx f(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = y'_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_p$ is the new $y$-value predicted by AB2, and $y'_p$ is the slope evaluated at $(x_\\text{new}, y_p).$\nSo, our three points are\n\n$$\n(-1,y'_{-1}), \\quad (0,y'), \\quad (1,y'_p).\n$$\n\nLet $q(\\theta)$ be the quadratic that passes through these three points. After simplifying, this quadratic can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\theta (\\theta +1){y}_{p}^{'}-(\\theta -1)(\\theta +1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}\\theta (\\theta -1){y}_{-1}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}+\\theta ){y}_{p}^{'}-({\\theta }^{2}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}-\\theta ){y}_{-1}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate this approximation from $0$ to $1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}q(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}+\\theta ){y}_{p}^{'}-({\\theta }^{2}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({\\theta }^{2}-\\theta ){y}_{-1}^{'})\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}){y}_{p}^{'}-(\\frac{1}{3}-1){y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}){y}_{-1}^{'}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}{y}_{p}^{'}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{12}{y}_{-1}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 g(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n\\approx \\int_0^1 q(\\theta) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x\n= \\dfrac1{12}(5y'_p + 8y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we use as the AM2 corrector. Putting it together, the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{'}+8{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5aa97723-f737-5f47-be29-c04919e09c0b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14479 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the AM2 Corrector"} :step/id #uuid "5990a7fa-57e1-5d5e-9ae3-587f865f923b" :step/math-academy-id 611755}] :activity/title "The ABM2 Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221477}]}
